The '''Trans-Guadalquivir Tunnel''' ({{langx|bl|Túnel Trans-Guadalquivir}}, {{langx|mxi|تُونِيلْ تْرَانْسْ - غْوَادَالَكِيبِيرْ|Túnel Trans-Ḡwadalquibír}}) is a {{convert|14.28|km|mich|adj=on}} [[W:Dual gauge|dual-gauge]] railway tunnel in [[Balisca]], with a {{convert|7.79|km|mich|adj=on}} segment running beneath the seabed of the [[Guadalquivir]], which separates the region of [[Piedmont]], on the Guadalquivir's northern shore, from the region [[Luzamontaña]] on it's southern shore. The tunnel's track level lies approximately {{convert|100|m|-1}} below the seabed and {{convert|240|m}} below sea level.

The Trans-Guadalquivir forms part of the standard-gauge [[Atlantic Axis high-speed rail line|HSR Atlantic Axis]] as well as the narrow-gauge [[Libánti Line]] operated by ''[[Transatlántida]]'' (BR Central), and forms a critical portion of the [[Trans-Atlántido Link]].

